<div id="fleet-root">
<p>Describe your fleet and workload; every model's coverage is computed from
the calibrated cost model <code>time&nbsp;=&nbsp;t₀&nbsp;+&nbsp;work&nbsp;÷&nbsp;(η&nbsp;×&nbsp;ceiling)</code>
— each model's work (bytes and FLOPs per token) is measured, t₀ and η are
pooled from the measured machines. Predictions are estimates; the error box
says how rough.</p>
<div class="note" id="fleet-honesty"></div>

<h2>Your fleet</h2>
<p class="muted">Counts are whatever unit you like (machines, %). Ceilings are
editable — the defaults are public-spec ballparks, not measurements. Memory is
what the model may use (VRAM for discrete cards). No Apple machine is in the
calibration set yet: those rows borrow the pooled GPU parameters and are pure
extrapolation until the first Mac submission lands.</p>
<table id="fleet-cohorts">
  <thead><tr><th>cohort</th><th>count</th><th>memory GB</th>
  <th>bandwidth GB/s</th><th>compute TFLOP/s</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody></tbody>
</table>

<h2>Workload &amp; experience tiers</h2>
<table><tbody>
  <tr><td>prompt tokens</td><td><input id="w-prompt" type="number" value="1500"></td>
      <td>output tokens</td><td><input id="w-out" type="number" value="256"></td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<table id="fleet-tiers">
  <thead><tr><th>tier</th><th>max seconds to first token</th><th>min tokens/s</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody></tbody>
</table>

<h2>Coverage</h2>
<p class="legend" id="fleet-legend"></p>
<table id="fleet-results">
  <thead><tr><th>model</th><th>coverage</th><th></th></tr></thead>
  <tbody></tbody>
</table>
<p class="muted">A cohort "fits" when weights + context + 1 GB headroom fit its
memory. Prompt-reading time omits the attention term (a few % at these
lengths).</p>

<details>
  <summary>calibration behind these predictions</summary>
  <p>Per measured lane, the affine fit and how well the parameters transfer
  (leave-one-out: each lane predicted using only the <em>other</em> lanes of
  its class; lanes with poor fits or unidentifiable rates borrow, never
  lend):</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>machine</th><th>provider</th><th>phase</th><th>t₀</th>
    <th>rate</th><th>η</th><th>R²</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
    {% for f in fits %}
      <tr><td>{{ f.machine }}</td><td>{{ f.provider }}</td><td>{{ f.kind }}</td>
          <td>{{ "%.1f"|format(f.t0_ms) }} ms</td><td>{{ f.rate }}</td>
          <td>{{ f.eta }}</td><td>{{ "%.2f"|format(f.r2) }}</td></tr>
    {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>machine</th><th>provider</th><th>phase</th>
    <th>lanes pooled</th><th>median error</th><th>worst</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
    {% for r in loo %}
      <tr><td>{{ r.machine }}</td><td>{{ r.provider }}</td><td>{{ r.kind }}</td>
          <td>{{ r.n_lanes_pooled }}</td>
          <td>{{ "%.0f"|format(r.median_err * 100) }}%</td>
          <td>{{ "%.0f"|format(r.worst_err * 100) }}%</td></tr>
    {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="muted">Known gaps the next backend iteration addresses: the CPU
  bandwidth probe under-measures (one lane's measured generation exceeds its
  "ceiling"), and the compute probe measures f16 while inference runs
  quantized kernels.</p>
</details>
</div>
